  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Let $X$ and $Y$ be discrete random variables, where $Y$ takes values $a$ and $b$ with probabilities $0.55$ and $0.45,$ respectively. You are given the following conditional expectations:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X∣Y=a]=6,\\,\\text{E}[X∣Y=b]=4\n$$\n\nWhat is $\\text{E}[X]?$",
  "worked_solution": "We are asked to compute the expected value of $X,$ given conditional expectations of $X$ based on the value of another random variable $Y.$ This is a direct application of the law of total expectation, which states\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\sum }_{y}\\text{E}[X∣Y=y]\\cdot P(Y=y).\n$$\n\nIn this problem, $Y$ takes two values:\n\n- $Y=a$ with probability $0.55,$ and $\\text{E}[X∣Y=a]=6$\n\n- $Y=b$ with probability $0.45,$ and $\\text{E}[X∣Y=b]=4$\n\nSo, we calculate our expected value as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\text{E}[X∣Y=a]\\cdot P(Y=a)+\\text{E}[X∣Y=b]\\cdot P(Y=b) \\\\  & =6\\cdot 0.55+4\\cdot 0.45 \\\\  & =3.3+1.8 \\\\  & =5.1\\end{aligned}\n$$",
